# Build context exclusions for frontend/Dockerfile (T-DO-2).
#
# The rule here is narrower than it looks, and deliberately so: **the context
# is the git-tracked working tree**, and the only things excluded are paths
# git already ignores (plus .git itself). That is not tidiness — it is what
# makes the container's `dist/` byte-identical to a local `npm run build`.
#
# Tailwind v4 has no `content` array. It detects sources automatically by
# walking the project and skipping what .gitignore skips, so *every tracked
# file in frontend/ is a build input*, including prose. Measured, not
# theorised: excluding README.md, CLAUDE.md, components.json and .oxlintrc.json
# from an earlier draft of this file dropped `.contents`, `.invert` and
# `.container` (with its five breakpoint media queries and the --breakpoint-md
# theme variable) out of the emitted stylesheet, because frontend/CLAUDE.md's
# sentences happen to contain those words. Adding this Dockerfile to the
# directory changed the local bundle by itself, for the same reason — the word
# "container" in a comment is a class name as far as the scanner is concerned.
#
# So anything excluded below must be something Tailwind would have skipped on
# the developer's machine anyway, or the two builds diverge. Dockerfile and
# .dockerignore are consequently *not* excluded, unusual as that looks: they
# are tracked, so they are scanned locally, so they have to be scanned here.
#
# That the stylesheet depends on documentation at all is a real defect in the
# frontend build config rather than a fact to design around — see T-DO-2's
# entry in tasks.md for the follow-up. This file reproduces the behaviour
# faithfully; it does not endorse it.

# Platform-specific and large: the win32 rollup/esbuild binaries are useless
# in a linux container and `npm ci` replaces the tree wholesale regardless.
# Excluding it is also what keeps `npm ci` honest — the install has to come
# from package-lock.json because there is nothing else to come from.
# (Tailwind never scans node_modules, so this costs no equivalence.)
node_modules

# The host's own build output — gitignored, so unscanned locally. It would be
# copied in only to be overwritten, and a stale file surviving into a
# comparison against the real output is the exact thing this task is checking.
dist
dist-ssr

# Playwright run output. Gitignored, so unscanned locally.
test-results
playwright-report
blob-report
playwright/.cache

# Vite env files. Gitignored (.env) or never read by the build (.env.example —
# Vite loads .env, .env.local and .env.[mode] only), so excluding them changes
# no output. They are excluded on purpose rather than by accident: DO-25 makes
# the API base URL a build argument, and the Dockerfile's ARG default is the
# single source for it. A .env in the context would restore a second source
# for one setting and let an uncommitted local file change what an image
# contains.
.env
.env.*

# History, not source. Tailwind skips it locally too.
.git
